; THE CONDOR — 1.2 m, 32 kg, TWR 12. The user's ask, 2026-08-10: *"a new quad
; with about the size of ~1m to 1.5m... I want to see how the momentum will carry
; with its reduced weight, while keeping the same thrust/weight ratio."*
;
; Named for the largest bird that actually flies, which is exactly its place on
; the ladder: kestrel (real, small) -> condor (real, large) -> roc (myth). One
; string changes the name if you want a different one.
;
; NOTHING HERE IS TUNED. Every number is DERIVED from the Kestrel by the laws
; recorded in `default_flight_roc.tres`, at this body size, and 32 kg is the same
; airframe density the Roc was given (18.5 kg/m^3, a deliberately light frame).
; It is a starting point for hands, not a verdict from a bench.
;
; ONE PREDICTION WORTH TESTING AGAINST, because it is the interesting half of the
; user's question and the arithmetic is counter-intuitive: at a fixed TWR,
; **reducing mass does not reduce momentum in any way you can feel**. Linear
; acceleration is g x TWR whatever the mass, and angular acceleration is
; (TWR / arm) with mass cancelled out entirely. What the smaller frame actually
; buys is:
;   - agility: 62% of the Kestrel's, against the Roc's 25% - 2.5x the Roc
;   - braking: drag deceleration goes as area/mass, so 1/S. This frame sheds
;     speed 2.5x faster than the Roc and the Kestrel sheds it 6.7x faster.
; So if the Condor feels less "heavy" than the Roc, it is the turn rate and the
; air resistance doing it, not the tonnage.
[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="FlightConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/flight_config.gd" id="1_cfg"]

